title: Probe corpora do not record which rows are environment-dependent, so a stable-looking verdict can be a foreign plugin's
updated_at: "2026-08-31T08:12:51Z"
---
Probe corpora record verdicts that depend on the probing machine's installed
plugin set, but nothing in the artifact says which rows those are. A reader
cannot tell an environment-dependent row from a stable one, and the writer
cannot tell either at write time.

## Why this is filed

Measured, not speculated. Re-probing the b052 corpus on a differently
configured machine flipped exactly 3 rows of 742, and all three were `Foo::*`
spellings that resolved through a namespace belonging to an installed plugin
that is no longer installed. 739 held. The b053 corpus re-probed 592 of 592
unchanged. So the effect is small, real, and invisible in the artifact.

The damage is not the flip itself. It is that a later reader cites such a row
as evidence of something it never showed: lane-b057's B070 comment cited those
`Foo::*` rows as workspace-declared witnesses, which they are not — they were a
foreign plugin's namespace. The corpus was correct; the provenance was missing,
so the reading went wrong.

## The design question, which is the actual content of this ticket

The weak form is cheap and nearly worthless on its own: one header line naming
the probing machine's plugin set at generation time, about 5 lines in each
writer.

The useful form is marking WHICH ROWS are environment-dependent, and the writer
**cannot compute that**. `Foo::VirtualKey` and `UI::VirtualKey` are
indistinguishable to it — both are namespace-qualified names that resolved.
Only re-probing on a differently configured machine separates them, which is
how the three flips were found, by hand.

So the knowledge has to live somewhere, and today it lives in
`PLUGIN_NAMESPACE_ARTEFACTS` — a Rust test constant in
`src/typecheck/resolver.rs:303`. That is the wrong home for a fact about a data
file: the corpus can be regenerated on another machine without the constant
changing, and nothing connects them.

Decide where it belongs before writing code. Candidates worth weighing:

- a per-row flag the writer sets from a declared environment manifest;
- a separate provenance sidecar keyed by row, so the corpus stays a pure
  measurement;
- the re-probe driver (`--reprobe`) emitting the marking as a diff between two
  machines, making environment-dependence a MEASURED property rather than a
  declared one.

The third is the only one that cannot go stale, because it derives the fact the
same way the fact was actually discovered. It also costs a second machine.

